cture.\n\n## 1. Introduction\n\nAll graphs considered in this paper are simple and finite. An antimagic labeling of a graph 𝐺 is a bijective mapping 𝜑 :𝐸⁡(𝐺) →{1,2,…,|𝐸⁡(𝐺)|} such that for any distinct vertices 𝑢 and 𝑣, the sum of labels on edges incident with 𝑢 differs from that for edges incident with 𝑣. If a graph 𝐺 admits an antimagic labeling, then we call 𝐺 antimagic. In 1990, Hartsfield and Ringel [10] introduced the antimagic labeling and made the following conjecture.\n\nConjecture 1\n\n## [10]\n\nEvery connected graph other than 𝐾2 is antimagic.\n\nThis conjecture has received much attention, but it remains open. Alon, Kaplan, Lev, Roditty, and Yuster [2] proved that there is an absolute constant 𝑐0 such that every graph 𝐺 with minimum degree 𝛿⁡(𝐺) ≥𝑐0⋅log⁡|𝑉⁡(𝐺)| is antimagic. They also proved that every graph 𝐺 with maximum degree 𝛥⁡(𝐺) ≥|𝑉⁡(𝐺)| −2 is antimagic. Later, Yilma [21] improved the result and verified that every graph 𝐺 with maximum degree 𝛥⁡(𝐺) ≥|𝑉⁡(𝐺)| −3 is antimagic. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nList of unsolved problems in mathematics (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_unsolved_problems_in_mathematics)\nciteturn7search18 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; * Sphere packing problems, including the density of the densest packing in dimensions other than 1, 2, 3, 8 and 24, and its asymptotic behavior for high dimensions. ... ### Graph theory ... * Cereceda's conjecture on the diameter of the space of colorings of degenerate graphs ... * Ringel's conjecture that the complete graph ${\\displaystyle K_{2n+1}}$ can be decomposed into ${\\displaystyle 2n+1}$ copies of any tree with ${\\displaystyle n}$ edges (Richard Montgomery, Benny Sudakov, Alexey Pokrovskiy, 2020). ... *  Hartsfield, Nora; Ringel, Gerhard (2013).\n* Blankenship-Oporowski conjecture on the book thickness of subdivisions (Vida Dujmović, David Eppstein, Robert Hickingbotham, Pat Morin, and David Wood, 2021). \n* Ringel's con